<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">talk</ansi>

The <ansi fg="command">talk</ansi> command starts a conversation with someone in the game
world, like a guard, a shopkeeper or a farmer. They speak first. Then a
numbered list of things you can say to them appears under their words.
Only you see the list. Other people in the room hear what you both say.

You can talk only to someone you can see clearly. In the dark, find a
light first.

<ansi fg="yellow">Usage: </ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">talk</ansi> <ansi fg="cyan"><name></ansi>
  <ansi fg="command">talk to</ansi> <ansi fg="cyan"><name></ansi>

<ansi fg="yellow">Choosing from the list: </ansi>

  Type the number of a line and press Enter to say it. They answer, and
  the list comes back so you can say more. To end the conversation, press
  Enter without typing anything, or choose <ansi fg="yellow">Leave</ansi>, the last line. Typing
  any other command also ends the conversation, and the game carries out
  that command.

  Some people have only a few words for you. Then no list appears.

<ansi fg="yellow">Quests: </ansi>

  A quest is a task someone asks you to do, like finding a lost item or
  chasing off a beast. You get a reward when you finish it. See
  <ansi fg="command">help quests</ansi>.

  When you choose a quest from the list, they tell you what they want.
  Then two lines appear: <ansi fg="yellow">1. Accept</ansi> takes the quest, and <ansi fg="yellow">2. Leave</ansi>
  says no for now. Pressing Enter also means Leave. Either way, the list
  comes back, and you can ask about the quest again later.

<ansi fg="yellow">Marks: </ansi>

  A mark in front of a line in the list tells you what it is:

  <ansi fg="yellow">[!]</ansi>  a new quest they can give you.
  <ansi fg="yellow">[?]</ansi>  a quest you can finish with them now. Choose it to report
       what you did.
  <ansi fg="yellow">[*]</ansi>  something important you have not heard yet.

  A line you have already heard shows in grey. You can choose it again.

  The same marks appear in front of a person's name when you look at a
  room, so you can see who has something for you before you talk to
  them. If a person has more than one, their name shows the first one
  in this order: <ansi fg="yellow">[?]</ansi>, <ansi fg="yellow">[!]</ansi>, <ansi fg="yellow">[*]</ansi>.

<ansi fg="yellow">Handing things over: </ansi>

  Some quests ask you to bring an item. When you have it, choose the
  quest's <ansi fg="yellow">[?]</ansi> line and you hand the item over. You can also type
  <ansi fg="command">give</ansi> <ansi fg="cyan"><item> <name></ansi>, and it works the same way. If a quest
  needs the item but you cannot hand it in yet (for example, you have not
  accepted the quest), they look at it and give it back to you.

<ansi fg="yellow">Examples: </ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">talk toke</ansi>
  <ansi fg="command">talk to tamsin</ansi>

<ansi fg="yellow">See Also: </ansi>
  <ansi fg="command">help quests</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help give</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help hint</ansi>
